15. What’s the difference between OAuth and JWT?
    - OAuth is an authorization framework that allows third-party applications to access user data without sharing credentials.
    - JWT (JSON Web Token) is a token format used to securely transmit information between parties.
    - OAuth can use JWT as a token format, but they serve different purposes.
    - OAuth is about granting access, while JWT is about securely transmitting information.

16. How does token expiration work?
    - Tokens have an expiration time (exp claim) to limit their validity.
    - After expiration, the token is no longer accepted for authentication.
    - Clients must refresh tokens using a refresh token or re-authenticate to obtain a new access token.
    - This helps mitigate risks associated with stolen tokens.

17. Can you invalidate a JWT token?
    - JWTs are stateless and cannot be invalidated server-side.
    - To handle token revocation, you can implement a blacklist or use short-lived tokens with refresh tokens.
    - Alternatively, you can change the signing key or add a version claim to force clients to re-authenticate.

18. How does refresh token mechanism work?
    - Refresh tokens are long-lived tokens used to obtain new access tokens without requiring user credentials.
    - When an access token expires, the client can send the refresh token to the server to request a new access token.
    - The server verifies the refresh token and issues a new access token if valid.
    - This allows for seamless user experience without frequent re-authentication.

19. How to implement infinite login application?
    - Use a combination of access tokens and refresh tokens.
    - Access tokens have a short expiration time (e.g., 15 minutes).
    - Refresh tokens have a longer expiration time (e.g., 30 days).
    - When the access token expires, the client uses the refresh token to obtain a new access token.
    - This allows users to stay logged in without frequent re-authentication while maintaining security.

20. Is Java 100% object-oriented?
    Java is not purely object-oriented because it supports primitive data types like int, char, etc., which are not
    objects. Also, features like static methods and variables allow us to use code without creating objects. Moreover,
    control structures like if-else are language constructs, not objects. These design choices were made for performance
    and simplicity, but they mean Java doesn’t follow the ‘everything is an object’ rule.

