38. Garbage Collector
    Serial GC
        - Uses a single thread for both minor and major collections.
        - Best for small applications
        - Pauses all application threads during GC
        - Uses Mark-Sweep or Mark-Compact algorithms

    Parallel GC
        - Uses multiple threads for minor collections
        - Best for multi-threaded applications on multi-core processors
        - Pauses all application threads during GC
        - Uses Mark-Sweep or Mark-Compact algorithms

    Concurrent Mark-Sweep (CMS) GC
        - Works concurrently with the application threads to minimize pause times.
        - Good for low-latency applications.
        - Uses Mark-Sweep algorithm
        - Drawbacks: Can lead to fragmentation.

     4. G1 GC (Garbage First)
        - Default since Java 9.
        - Splits the heap into regions and collects them in parallel.
        - Aims for predictable, low pause times while still being throughput-friendly.

    Minor GC
        - Cleans the Young Generation (Eden + Survivor spaces)
        - Happens frequently, usually when Eden space is full
        - Fast, as it deals with short-lived objects
        - Uses algorithms like Copying or Scavenge

    Major GC
        - Cleans the Old Generation (Tenured space)
        - Happens less frequently, usually when Old Gen is full
        - Slower than Minor GC, as it deals with long-lived objects
        - Uses algorithms like Mark-Sweep or Mark-Compact

