40. A Bloom Filter is a probabilistic data structure used to test whether an element is a member of a set.
    - It can say definitely not in the set.
    - False positives - The system says "Yes, it exists" but in reality, it does not.
    - Very space-efficient and fast (constant time insert + lookup).

    🔹 How it Works
    - A bit array of length m (all bits start as 0).
    - k hash functions that map an element to k positions in the bit array.
    - Insert(element):
        a. Hash the element using each of the k functions.
        b. Set the corresponding bits to 1.
    - Query(element):
        a. Hash the element with the same k functions.
        b. If all corresponding bits are 1, → "maybe present".
        c. If any bit is 0, → "definitely not present".

